 complications  the separation of powers presents for  american foreign policy  and in particular for the exercise of  the war power  yet the constitution is more than just  an invitation to the two political  branches  to struggle for control over the war  power  rather the constitution structures and  empowers the congress  to be a representative and deliberative  institution with final authority over  whether to move the nation from a state  of peace  to a state of war and the same  constitution created a single energetic  executive and empowered it to act  decisively and often secretly  to protect american lives and interest  in a dangerous world  in the decades after world war ii we had  gotten away from the notion  before time of most of you of course  we've gotten away from the notion  that congress had to formally auth the  congress  uh had to formally authorize any  military actions  that the president wished to undertake  that as many people argue that congress  never had to authorize  anything the president could always just  go first so even a major engagement like  the korean war  in the early 50s which was funded by  congress and the draft was extended by  congress so congress supported what  truman was trying to do in the war  it was never actually authorized by  congress  thus when things weren't going well in  korea after a couple of years and the  war became  unpopular people started calling it mr  truman's war  mr truman's war because the congress  hadn't  in an official way authorized it and  bought into it  even though the later vietnam war was  authorized by congress in the gulf of  tonka resolution 1964  that war engended a belief among many  that over the years  congress had ceded to the president its  proper constitutional responsibility  to decide whether to go to war when war  did not  already exist consequently  the last three major military and  military engagements undertaken by the  united states  this is interesting and quite in  contrast to korea have all been  formally authorized by congress so what  am i talking without a formal  declaration of war what am i talking  about  the first gulf war of 1991 you have  formal  they're called aumfs authorization for  use of military force  they evolved they the often resolutions  but they have full force of law  so the first gulf war was authorized by  an aumf  the war against al qaeda and the taliban  regime  after the attacks of 9 11 was authorized  by an aumf  and the more recent war against saddam  hussein's regime in iraq  that started in 2003 was authorized by  an aumf  now where the war powers act or war  power's resolution in 1973  fits into all this obviously there's no  time to discuss now  maybe in the q a we can talk about it  so very final thought on the war powers  controversy  scholars policy makers and others tend  to be either pro-constitution  or pro-presidency but what we ought to  be  is not the advocate of one institution  or the other  but rather advocates of the constitution  we ought to be pro-constitution  not pro-presidency or pro-congress on  these matters  and in some cases indeed in many cases  the constitution will  side with the with presidential  initiative  without prior approval by congress but  in  others the constitution says to the  president  you should not act unless and until  congress approves in my view  the wisdom embedded in the  constitution's provisions  never ceases to surprise thank you very  [Applause]  much
 

